To make this solution more efficient for handling very large texts, we can use memory-mapped files instead of loading the entire contents into memory at once. This allows us to read and process the files in smaller chunks, which reduces memory usage and improves performance.

Here's how you can modify the previous implementation to use memory-mapped files:

```matlab
% Read text1 and text2 using memory-mapped files
filename1 = 'Text1.txt';
filename2 = 'Text2.txt';
mmFile1 = mmopen(filename1, 'r');
mmFile2 = mmopen(filename2, 'r');
mmMap1 = mmmap(mmFile1);
mmMap2 = mmmap(mmFile2);

% Convert text to lowercase and split into words
words1 = strfun(@(x) lower(strsplit(x)), mmMap1);
words2 = strfun(@(x) lower(strsplit(x)), mmMap2);

% Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1(:), words2(:));

% Close and unmap the memory-mapped files
mmclose(mmFile1);
mmunmap(mmMap1);
mmclose(mmFile2);
mmunmap(mmMap2);

% Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords(:));
```

In this modified implementation, we open both text files as memory-mapped files using `mmopen()`. We then map the files using `mmmap()`, which returns a cell array containing the mapped data. We use `strfun()` to apply the string operations (lowercasing and splitting into words) to each chunk of text in parallel. Finally, we close and unmap the memory-mapped files using `mmclose()` and `mmunmap()`.

By using memory-mapped files, we avoid reading the entire contents of the files into memory at once, which is especially useful when dealing with very large texts. This allows us to process the text more efficiently and with less memory usage.

