To further optimize efficiency for handling a large amount of text, you can consider using text processing libraries like Text Analytics Toolbox or NLP Toolbox for MATLAB. These toolboxes offer more advanced functionalities such as tokenization, stop word removal, stemming, and lemmatization, which can help reduce the number of unique words and improve computational performance. Here's an updated implementation using the Text Analytics Toolbox:

```matlab
% Load Text Analytics Toolbox
license('toolbox_nlp')

% Read texts from files
[text1, ~] = readDocument('Text1.docx');
[text2, ~] = readDocument('Text2.docx');

% Tokenize texts
tokens1 = tokenize(text1);
tokens2 = tokenize(text2);

% Remove stop words and punctuation marks
stopwords = {'a', 'an', 'and', 'are', 'as', 'at', 'but', 'by', 'for', 'if', 'in', 'is', 'it', 'no', 'not', 'of', 'on', 'or', 'such', 'the', 'their', 'then', 'there', 'these', 'they', 'this', 'to', 'was', 'will', 'with'};
tokens1 = removeStopwords(tokens1, stopwords);
tokens2 = removeStopwords(tokens2, stopwords);

% Stem tokens using Porter stemmer
stems1 = stem(tokens1, 'porter');
stems2 = stem(tokens2, 'porter');

% Find unique stems that don't appear in stems2
uniqueStems = setdiff(stems1, stems2);

% Print unique stems
disp(uniqueStems);
```

This implementation uses the `readDocument()` function from the Text Analytics Toolbox to read the documents in Microsoft Word format (`.docx`) directly. Then it applies stop word removal and stemming using the `removeStopwords()` and `stem()` functions respectively. The resulting unique stems can be printed using `disp()`. Note that this implementation may require a license for the Text Analytics Toolbox. If you have a large dataset, you might want to consider converting the documents to a plain text format

