To address the `InputMismatchException` in your Java program, you can add input validation to ensure that the user enters an integer when prompting for numerical inputs. Here's how you can modify your existing code to handle this exception:

```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        int num1, num2, result;

        System.out.print("Enter first number: ");
        while (true) {
            try {
                num1 = scanner.nextInt();
                break;
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // consume invalid input
            }
        }

        System.out.print("Enter second number: ");
        while (true) {
            try {
                num2 = scanner.nextInt();
                break;
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // consume invalid input
            }
        }

        result = num1 + num2;
        System.out.println("The sum is: " + result);

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

In this modified code, I've added a `while` loop around the input statements to keep asking the user for input until a valid integer is entered. Inside the loops, I'm wrapping the `nextInt()` method call inside a `try-catch` block to catch the `InputMismatchException`. If an exception is thrown, I print an error message and consume the invalid input using the `nextLine()` method. This ensures that the next input statement does not read the invalid input again.

With these changes, your program will now be more robust and handle invalid input gracefully.

