To implement bubble sort in C++ for sorting an integer array, you can follow these steps:

1. Define a function called `bubbleSort` that takes an array and its size as parameters.
2. Inside the function, define two nested loops. The outer loop will iterate through all elements from the first to the second-last position. The inner loop will compare adjacent elements and swap them if they are in the wrong order.
3. After each iteration of the outer loop, check if any swaps were made. If not, the array is already sorted and there's no need to continue iterating.
4. Minimize memory usage by passing the array and size as arguments instead of creating new variables to store them.
5. Avoid potential errors by handling edge cases like empty arrays or arrays with only one element.

Here's the implementation:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped;
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < n - I - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) {
            break; // Array is already sorted
        }
    }
}
```

