It's possible that the server is preventing you from including an Authorization header when making cross-origin requests due to CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) restrictions. When the server sets 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' to '\*', it allows any origin to access the resource, but this doesn't necessarily mean that all request headers will be allowed as well. In fact, by default, CORS restrictions only allow certain request headers like 'Content-Type' and 'Accept'.

To include an Authorization header in a cross-origin request, you need to ensure that the server has explicitly allowed it through CORS settings. This can be done by adding 'Authorization' to the 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers' list on the server side. Here's an example of how this might look like in Node.js using the 'cors' middleware:

```javascript
const cors = require('cors');
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Enable CORS for all routes
app.use(cors({
  // Allow any origin, but specify which headers are allowed
  origin: '*',
  optionsSuccessStatus: 200,
  allowedHeaders: ['Authorization']
}));

// Handle your API endpoint here
app.get('/api/resource', (req, res) => {
  // Use req.headers.Authorization to get the Authorization header
});

// Start the server
app.listen(3000);
```

In this example, we're allowing any origin ('*') and specifying that the 'Authorization' header is allowed. You'll need to replace '*' with the specific origins you want to allow if you prefer more fine-grained control over CORS. Once you've made these changes on the server side, you should be able to include the Authorization header in your cross-origin requests.

Note that some servers may already have CORS enabled by default, so you may not need to add this code. It's always best to check the documentation or consult with the server administrator to confirm the current CORS configuration.

