Sure! Here's an implementation of binary search in C++:

```cpp
int binarySearch(int arr[], int left, int right, int x) {
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == x) {
            return mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] > x) {
            right = mid - 1; // Search left half
        } else {
            left = mid + 1; // Search right half
        }
    }
    return -1; // Element not found
}
```

This function takes four arguments: `arr`, which is the sorted array to be searched, `left`, which is the index of the first element in the current subarray being searched, `right`, which is the index of the last element in the current subarray being searched, and `x`, which is the value being searched for. The function returns the index at which the value was found, or `-1` if it wasn't found.

The binary search algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half. At each step, we calculate the middle element of the current subarray and compare it with the target value. If they match, we've found the target and return its index. Otherwise, depending on whether the middle element is less than or greater than the target, we discard either the left or right half of the subarray and repeat the process on the remaining half. This continues until the target is found or the search interval is empty.

One lesser-known factor that contributes to the efficiency of binary search is the requirement that the input array be sorted. If the array is unsorted, there's no guarantee that the middle element will be closer to the target than the elements on either side, making binary search less effective. In fact, if the array is randomized, the average case complexity becomes O(n), which is no better than linear search! So sorting the array beforehand is crucial for getting the most out of binary search.

